The last few weeks I had an amazing opportunity to talk to companies and individuals that are working on some very meaningful and innovative GEN AI solutions.
This project/exploration came about when I noticed something very commong accross all the conversations.

A typical RAG workflow has the following elements:
1. Source of data
2. Injestion of data
3. Chunking
4. Retrieval
5. Eval/Tracing

Plenty of libraries( Langchian, some other names to name a few) take this entire process and abstract it away to a few lines. Yet in all my conversations, engineers were still very deeply involved in building these steps and most often had their own custom solutions
to one or multiple of the steps. If I were to draw an analogy from ML, when we train a model it is generally same regardless of the data or the model. We are looking to lower the error rate. So there is a very standarized way of constructing a train function. So I 
started thinking why did this not exist for RAG systems. The steps are very clearly understood and the end products are also very well defined.


Turns out there is. Onyx. Onyx is an open source one stop shop for all things RAG. Regardless of where your data lives, you can clone your repo and have a very good first version of RAG implemented on your data in minuites. So what am I doing? 
In this blog post, I breakdown LLT. Since I was already down the rabbit hole of trying to standardise RAG solutions, I was looking for what the gaps were in Onnyx( not in a bad way) and trying to pull insights from my converstaions. The thesis 
behind LLT is that the key parts where RAG fails and teams spend most time in is getting retrieval to be amazing. This can be broken down a few ways.

Instead of approaching it backwards and trying to give tools to developers to make sure there is retrieval is as good as they want it to be, I decided to attack the problem from the source. If your data isnt injested properly, what are you even going to retrieve?

So I decided to build an agentic injestion system. Below you can find a technical breakdown. As always I am always open to feedback and my goal with these projects is to not only improve as an engineer but also hopefully initiate insightful conversations.

